          guideText: `{name}, Indigenous peoples are the original inhabitants of lands colonized by European powers (and in some cases by other expansionist states): the peoples of the Americas before 1492, of Australia before 1788, of Aotearoa New Zealand before 1840, of the Arctic regions, of significant parts of Africa and Asia where European powers arrived later. Today approximately 476 million Indigenous people live across roughly 90 countries (per UN Permanent Forum on Indigenous Issues, 2023), maintaining distinct cultures, languages, governance systems, and territorial connections despite five centuries of colonization, dispossession, attempted assimilation, and outright destruction. Today five voices on Indigenous experience, agency, and sovereignty: the colonial-destruction voice (what was lost); the survivance voice (what was preserved and renewed through active resistance); the sovereignty voice (Indigenous nations as continuing political entities); the decolonial voice (Indigenous knowledge as theoretical and methodological challenge to Western frameworks); and the contemporary politics voice (current Indigenous movements and unresolved struggles). Engaging multiple voices is essential because Indigenous experience isn't single story; it includes both the documented catastrophe of colonization and the continuing existence of Indigenous nations, cultures, and political movements that Western framings often miss.`,
